---

It still took until 1746 for Spanish explorers to sail completely to the top of the gulf to confirm that the water did not continue to the ocean. The following year, in 1747, King Ferdinand VI of Spain formally declared to all the world that California was not an island. Part of the reason it took so long to correct maps of California was that harsh weather and ocean conditions made exploring the Pacific northwest difficult. And even after cartographers knew that California was a peninsula, it still took time to replace or modify old copper plates for printing. 

